# Duetto No. 4 in A minor, BWV 805 (J. S. Bach)
#
# Character: the last of the four Duetti from Clavier-Uebung III
# (1739): a strict two-part fugue alla breve, in the old style. The
# subject, eight bars long, enters alone in the bass; it climbs by step
# and half step (A, C, E, then F, E, D, E, F-sharp, G, A, B-flat, A,
# G-sharp) and ends in a figure of repeated broken eighths. Serious and
# steady, in two beats to the bar.
#
# Time signature: 2/2, 108 bars (Mutopia Bach-Duet4.mid /
# Bach-Duet4.ly). Bar 1 starts with a half rest and the subject's
# first note on the second beat; that is the score's bar 1, not an
# upbeat. No repeats.
#
# Tempo: half note = 72, about 3:05. Track times (listing order taken as
# BWV 802-805): Ton Koopman (organ, Teldec, 1996) 3:01, about half =
# 72; Rosalyn Tureck (piano, 1959) 3:10, about 68; Helmut Walcha
# (harpsichord, 1951) 3:17, about 66; Ralph Kirkpatrick (harpsichord,
# 1952) 2:15 and (Archiv, 1959) 2:22, about 96 and 91. Source: the
# bach-cantatas.com discographies. 72 follows Koopman, close to Tureck
# and Walcha: alla breve but not hurried, so the chromatic lines are
# heard; the eighths are 12 frames each.
#
# Structure (my reading of the score and the MIDI; the recurring
# passages were found by comparing bars in the MIDI):
#   1-8    subject, bass, alone, A minor.
#   9-16   answer, top, on E; the bass's countersubject.
#   17-32  episode: chromatic sequences falling by step, the lines in
#          contrary motion; cadence in A minor at 32-33.
#   33-40  subject, top, A minor (the countersubject in the bass).
#   41-48  answer in the bass, toward E minor.
#   49-69  episodes through C major, G major and E minor, in sixteenth
#          and eighth figures; back on the dominant of A at 69.
#   70-77  the answer in the top on E again, the same as bars 9-16.
#   78-96  the episode of 17-32 returns with the parts exchanged.
#   97-103 the subject in the bass, A minor, for the last time.
#   104-108 closing, final cadence in A minor.
#
# Plan: terraced by the fugue's sections: entries full, episodes a step
# softer, the last bass entry fullest. The subject or answer brought
# forward where it enters against the other part. Bass notes below B2
# are lifted an octave by the converter; few are affected.

tempo 72 2

voice mid 0           # two voices only

dyn 1 mf              # bars 1-16: subject and answer
lead 9-16 top         # answer, top, on E
dyn 17 mp             # bars 17-32: first episode
broaden 32 0.95       # bar 32: into the A minor cadence
dyn 33 mf             # bars 33-48: subject top, answer bass
lead 33-40 top
lead 41-48 bottom
dyn 49 mp             # bars 49-69: the modulating episodes
broaden 69 0.95       # bar 69: the dominant, before the return
dyn 70 mf             # bars 70-77: answer in the top again
lead 70-77 top
dyn 78 mp             # bars 78-96: the episode with parts exchanged
dyn 97 f              # bars 97-108: last entry in the bass, the close
lead 97-103 bottom

rit 2 0.75            # bars 107-108: the final cadence
